! The disk (low-memory) form-factor path: the same urea HAR as
! tests/hart/urea_hart_STO-3G, but with --disk-sfs t.
!
! WHY THIS TEST EXISTS. Until 2026-08-02 this path had never been executed by
! anything -- no job file enables use_disk_SFs, and fragHAR forces it on but
! routes to LS_fit_fragHAR_disk instead, so MOLECULE.HAR:make_LS_mx had zero
! coverage. It accumulated SIX defects, none of which any test could see:
!
!   1. d_Fa declared OUT rather than INOUT -- intent(out) deallocates an
!      allocatable on entry, so an unallocated array reached d_F_abs_dX's
!      non-allocatable dummy. SIGSEGV on the first call.
!   2. update_fit_info never called -- .fit_finished was tested but nothing set
!      it. Infinite loop.
!   3. initialize_fit_data called once per LS iteration, re-creating X_fit and
!      resetting chi2_fit0 = huge() every pass.
!   4. X_fit re-seeded from the atoms once per ATOM per iteration. X_fit is
!      authoritative and the atoms follow it, so this discarded the refinement.
!   5. .atom never refreshed from crystal.fragment_atom inside the loop, so the
!      design matrix was rebuilt from the ORIGINAL coordinates every iteration.
!   6. the site occupancy was missing from the form factors (latent: every
!      refining test structure is fully occupied).
!
! Plus a seventh in the hand-inlined copy of CRYSTAL:initialize_fit_data that
! make_LS_mx used to carry, which assigned a possibly-unallocated allocatable.
!
! WHAT IT CHECKS. That the disk path still runs to completion and still gives
! the in-core answer. Compare with tests/hart/urea_hart_STO-3G, same structure,
! same basis, same grid:
!
!             in core     on disk
!   R(F)     0.037992    0.037995
!   N_r/N_p   817 / 27    817 / 27
!   GoF      7.037680    7.038112
!   Scale    0.981084    0.981080
!
! Four significant figures, which is as much as this comparison is worth.
!
! --max-iterations 3 is deliberate, and does two things at once. It cuts the job
! from ~5 minutes to ~22 s, and it gives a BETTER answer: left to run, the fit
! oscillates for 264 rows and finally stops on "chi2 has increased" having drifted
! away from the optimum (R(F) 0.038220). Capped, it stops while still near it.
! So the reference records a deliberately UNCONVERGED fit -- the log says
! "WARNING: refinement stopped: too many iterations." and that line is expected.
! The point of this test is that the code path runs and produces the right
! numbers, not that the refinement is converged; tests/hart/urea_hart_STO-3G
! covers convergence on the in-core path.
!
! --residual-cube f likewise: the cube is the single most expensive thing a
! refinement writes and nothing here checks it.
!
! --grid-accuracy very_low would be cheaper still but SEGFAULTS (rc=139 after
! 99 s) -- an open item in DEFERRED.md -- so `low` is used.

   program: hart
   args:    --job urea --basis STO-3G --grid-accuracy low --residual-cube f --max-iterations 3 --disk-sfs t urea_init.cif
   input:   urea_init.cif
   output:  urea.out
   output:  urea.archive.cif
   delete:  urea.err
   delete:  urea.HBB.cif2
   delete:  urea.archive.fcf
   delete:  urea.archive.fco
   delete:  urea.cartesian.cif2
   delete:  urea.fcf6
   delete:  urea.fractional.cif1
   delete:  urea.correlation-coefficients
   delete:  urea.MOs,r
   delete:  urea.MO_energies,r
   delete:  urea.density_mx,r
   delete:  C-SFs.unknown,ascii
   delete:  N1-SFs.unknown,ascii
   delete:  O-SFs.unknown,ascii
   delete:  H1-SFs.unknown,ascii
   delete:  H3-SFs.unknown,ascii
   delete:  stdout.F_z_vs_stl
   delete:  stdout.Delta_F_vs_stl
   delete:  stdout.F_z_vs_F_exp
   delete:  stdout.QQ_plot_with_hkl
   delete:  stdout.QQ_plot.gnuplot
